+# Decision: Change Delivery Mechanism
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard serves up to 800 simultaneous staff browsers across forty weekend markets, with 10–25 active editors per busy market averaging two status changes per minute and opening-time bursts up to 20 edits per second. Editors submit commands over HTTPS, and each committed edit receives a single authoritative event revision.
+
+Connected browsers must display committed changes within 15 seconds under normal connectivity, recover the latest board after disconnects lasting up to 20 minutes, and surface connectivity failures visibly rather than leaving stale state looking current. The delivery mechanism chosen at the root determines how `change-store` indexes and publishes revisions, how `delivery` serves updates and manages connections, and how `browser-sync` subscribes, detects drops, and catches up.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+### Weighted Criteria
+
+- **Vendor independence and predictable operating cost (major)** — Avoid recurring per-connection or per-message vendor billing and avoid coupling the core delivery contract (`change-store`, `delivery`, `browser-sync`) to a proprietary realtime provider's SDK, token model, and channel semantics.
+- **Update freshness after commit (moderate)** — Minimize the delay between a committed edit and its appearance on connected staff browsers, especially during opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second when coordinators act on stall closures and power problems in real time.
+- **Disconnect detection and catch-up fidelity (moderate)** — Support the tenet that connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board, and cleanly recover up to 20 minutes of missed revisions around the single authoritative event revision tenet.
+- **Server and operational simplicity (moderate)** — Minimize stateful connection management, session routing, and failure-recovery machinery that the team must build and operate to sustain 99.5% availability during published event hours.
+- **Steady-state read load on the change store (minor)** — Limit redundant database or cache queries generated by idle browsers when no edits are occurring.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-Second HTTPS Polling
+
+- **Description** — Browsers run a timer that requests `GET /events/{id}/changes?since={rev}` every five seconds over stateless HTTPS. The `delivery` component is a standard request/response handler that queries `change-store` by `(event_id, revision > since)` and returns any committed revisions (or a full snapshot if `since` falls outside the replay window). `browser-sync` advances its local cursor on each response and marks connectivity degraded when consecutive polls fail or time out.
+- **Demonstration** — `GET /events/evt-42/changes?since=108` → `200 OK { "revisions": [109, 110], "latest": 110 }`.
+- **Analysis**
+  - *Vendor independence and predictable operating cost*: **strong** — Uses standard stateless HTTPS handlers with no third-party realtime vendor or per-connection fees.
+  - *Update freshness after commit*: **partial** — Meets the 15-second goal with an average ~2.5-second (up to 5-second plus network RTT) lag, but introduces multi-second delay during opening-time coordination bursts.
+  - *Disconnect detection and catch-up fidelity*: **partial** — Cursor catch-up is uniform across normal polls and post-disconnect recovery, but disconnect detection lags by at least one poll interval plus request timeout before the browser can surface a connectivity warning.
+  - *Server and operational simplicity*: **strong** — Completely stateless request/response operation; no long-lived socket registries, heartbeat loops, or connection-draining coordination during deploys.
+  - *Steady-state read load on the change store*: **weak** — 800 connected browsers generate ~160 requests per second continuously even when a market has zero edits, requiring read caching or indexed cursor scans sized for constant background traffic.
+- **Summary** — Lowest operational complexity and zero vendor coupling, at the cost of multi-second update lag and continuous background read load.
+
+### Self-Hosted WebSockets
+
+- **Description** — Browsers establish a persistent WebSocket session to the `delivery` service for their active event, supplying their last known revision on connect (`?since={rev}`). `change-store` commits each authoritative revision to a replayable revision log and publishes the committed revision to `delivery` (via an outbox or pub/sub feed). `delivery` validates subscription access, replays any revisions between `since` and the live stream (or directs the client to load a snapshot if the gap exceeds the 20-minute replay window), fans out live revisions to subscribed sockets, and runs heartbeat pings. `browser-sync` tracks socket lifecycle events and missed heartbeats to surface connectivity loss and drives exponential-backoff reconnects with `since={last_seen_rev}`.
+- **Demonstration** — `WS /events/evt-42/stream?since=108` → server sends missed `[109, 110]`, then pushes `111` immediately on commit.
+- **Analysis**
+  - *Vendor independence and predictable operating cost*: **strong** — Runs on self-hosted infrastructure using standard WebSocket protocols; no external realtime provider fees or proprietary client SDK coupling.
+  - *Update freshness after commit*: **strong** — Pushes committed revisions to connected browsers in subsecond latency, keeping all 800 browsers immediately aligned during 20-edits-per-second opening bursts.
+  - *Disconnect detection and catch-up fidelity*: **strong** — Socket close events and heartbeat timeouts allow `browser-sync` to surface connectivity failures promptly, while revision-tagged replay over the same stream guarantees ordered catch-up without gap or duplication.
+  - *Server and operational simplicity*: **weak** — Requires operating stateful WebSocket servers for 800 concurrent connections, managing connection draining and reconnect storms across deploys, and coordinating reliable publication and replay between `change-store` and `delivery`.
+  - *Steady-state read load on the change store*: **strong** — Idle browsers perform zero store reads; `change-store` is queried only on commits and on initial connect or reconnect replay.
+- **Summary** — Delivers subsecond updates and zero vendor coupling, trading away stateless simplicity for in-house connection capacity, publication reliability, and reconnect/replay engineering.
+
+### Hosted Realtime Service
+
+- **Description** — Browsers subscribe to event channels managed by an external realtime provider (such as Pusher, Ably, or AWS AppSync/IoT) using short-lived capability tokens minted by `delivery`. When `change-store` commits a revision, an outbox worker in `delivery` publishes the revision payload to the provider's API and reconciles publication failures. `browser-sync` embeds the provider's client SDK to receive live messages, observe connection state, and request missed history or trigger a snapshot fetch against `delivery` on reconnect.
+- **Demonstration** — `POST /events/evt-42/subscription-token` → browser connects to provider channel `event:evt-42` via provider SDK; `delivery` publishes revision `111` to provider REST/socket API.
+- **Analysis**
+  - *Vendor independence and predictable operating cost*: **weak** — Introduces recurring per-connection and per-message charges across 800 concurrent browsers and couples `delivery` token issuance, outbox reconciliation, and `browser-sync` client code to a specific vendor's API and failure modes.
+  - *Update freshness after commit*: **strong** — Delivers subsecond push updates after `delivery` forwards the committed revision to the provider.
+  - *Disconnect detection and catch-up fidelity*: **partial** — The provider SDK surfaces socket state, but authoritative revision catch-up either depends on provider-specific history retention limits or requires a secondary fallback path back to MarketBoard's own `delivery` and `change-store` nodes whenever a commit is in flight to the provider or a disconnect exceeds provider channel memory.
+  - *Server and operational simplicity*: **partial** — Offloads raw socket termination and fan-out scaling for 800 browsers, but adds transactional outbox reconciliation (handling commits that succeed locally while provider publication fails or times out) and external credential lifecycle management.
+  - *Steady-state read load on the change store*: **strong** — Idle browsers sit on the provider's infrastructure and generate no read load against `change-store`.
+- **Summary** — Offloads socket connection scaling while achieving subsecond latency, but incurs recurring vendor costs, provider lock-in, and split authority between local commits and external channel state.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Chosen option: Self-Hosted WebSockets.**
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets win because they deliver subsecond updates and quiet steady-state store load without tying MarketBoard's architecture or operating budget to a third-party realtime vendor. While five-second HTTPS polling is simpler to operate, it imposes multi-second lag during opening-time bursts and 160 RPS of continuous polling across 800 browsers. While a hosted realtime service offloads socket termination, it scores poorly on the major criterion of vendor independence and still requires MarketBoard to build outbox reconciliation and authoritative catch-up paths to uphold the tenet that *a committed edit has one authoritative event revision regardless of its delivery route*. Accepting the operational work of connection management and replay in-house keeps revision authority, delivery, and catch-up under one cohesive protocol and supports the tenet that *connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board* via direct socket lifecycle and heartbeat control.
+
+### Implications
+
+- **Foreclosed**: Stateless-only `delivery` deployment; deploys and health checks must account for long-lived WebSocket connections and reconnect waves.
+- **Downstream obligations**:
+  - `change-store` (`docs/intent/change-store/change-store-design.md`): Must persist ordered, replayable event revisions covering at least the 20-minute disconnect recovery window (and 7-day retention goal) and provide reliable publication notification so committed revisions are never silently dropped between commit and socket fan-out.
+  - `delivery` (`docs/intent/delivery/delivery-design.md`): Must authenticate and authorize WebSocket subscriptions, track active sessions per event up to 800 concurrent browsers, splice replay from `since` into live revision fan-out without gaps, and run heartbeats.
+  - `browser-sync` (`docs/intent/browser-sync/browser-sync-design.md`): Must manage the WebSocket connection lifecycle, surface disconnected/reconnecting states immediately in the UI, reconnect with jittered backoff supplying the last applied revision, and fall back to a full board snapshot when the disconnect gap exceeds the replay window.
